# The machine looking at itself, and this collection looking at
# itself.  Some of these meet os9exec's limits rather than their
# own -- where they do, the screen says so.

shot    about
cap     The collection's own guide: `about hack' answers what a program is,
cap     where it came from, what it opens and whether its source survived.
run     about hack
wait    4

shot    about-tar
for     about
cap     about answers for any program on the disk, out of the same four
cap     files the web catalogue is built from.
run     about elvis
wait    5

shot    aprocs
cap     A process monitor -- and it cannot run here.  It reads the system
cap     globals through F$SetSys, which os9exec does not implement, and
cap     aborts.  This is a limit of the emulator, not of the program.
run     aprocs
wait    5
kill

shot    argproc_demo
cap     argproc_demo shows RICO's command-line parser.  It is starred: with
cap     no cio it stops before it starts, and this is what that looks like.
run     argproc_demo -a -b=2 file
wait    6

shot    ask
for     ask ynad wisecrack
cap     `ask' is not the shell-script prompt its name suggests: it is the
cap     CLIENT half of a pair.  `wisecrack' is the server -- start it in
cap     the background and every `ask' pulls one line out of it through
cap     /PIPE/txtpipe.  Alone, either one is silent, which is why every
cap     sweep of this disk scored BOTH as mute.  The slogans are from a
cap     German OS-9 seminar in 1992.  `ynad' beside it is YNAD, Yet
cap     Another Name & Address program, also not a dialogue.
run     ask "nothing is feeding the pipe yet"
wait    5
run     wisecrack &
wait    5
run     ask "and now?"
wait    5
run     ask "again"
wait    5
run     ask "once more"
wait    6

shot    bcheck
cap     bcheck COUNTS BRACKETS in a source file and reports a mismatch --
cap     it is not the boot-file checker DOC/INDEX called it, which is what
cap     its own usage line says when you ask.  Run over a file it says
cap     nothing at all when the brackets balance.  Given a file it numbers
cap     every line with the nesting depth it is at.
run     bcheck -?
wait    5
run     bcheck /dd/SRC/keep/about.c | head -n 10
wait    10

shot    bigsetter
cap     A Modula-2 set-operations demonstration -- proof the Modula
cap     compiler's output runs here.
run     bigsetter
wait    8

shot    bootgen
for     bootgen bcheck bsplt68 modbuster
cap     The boot-file tools: bootgen makes an OS9Boot, bcheck checks one
cap     and bsplt68 takes one apart.  There is no boot file here -- os9exec
cap     IS the kernel -- so these are for your own hardware.
run     bootgen -?
wait    8
run     bsplt68 -?
wait    8

shot    btree
cap     A B-tree demonstration, building and walking an index.
run     btree
wait    8

shot    clock
for     clock date setime setime2 setyear fixyear
cap     `clock' does not run here -- `Popen error 216', it wants a pipe it
cap     cannot open.  What follows is the pair that DO work: setime2 and
cap     setyear, two of the three Y2K repairs on this disk, which exist
cap     because `date' answers 2100.
run     clock
wait    6
run     setime2 -?
wait    6
run     setyear -?
wait    6
run     date
wait    6
run     setime -?
wait    6
run     fixyear -?
wait    6

shot    config
cap     config reports this machine's C type properties as the #defines a
cap     portable program would want.
run     config
wait    5

shot    cpu
cap     A CPU speed test.  It takes os9exec down with it every time -- the
cap     emulator exits mid-run, which is why the screen stops where it does.
run     cpu
wait    10

shot    dam
cap     The disk allocation map: one character per cluster across a 253 MB
cap     volume, so you can see where the collection sits on it.
run     dam /dd | head -n 21
wait    10

shot    date
cap     date, and the Y2K repairs beside it.  It prints the year 2100 for
cap     2026 -- one of the reasons setyear and fixyear are on this disk.
run     date
wait    4
run     date -t
wait    4
run     today
wait    5

shot    demo
cap     demo, the egetopt option-parsing example, showing how it read the
cap     command line it was given.
run     demo -a -b -c=7 one two
wait    6

shot    devprc
for     devprc ptxminst t t_trtest
cap     devprc shows which device belongs to which process.  Rebuilt here:
cap     the archived module had been patched and would not run.  `ptxminst'
cap     installs Ptxm, and `t' and `t_trtest' are the trap-handler test
cap     pair; the three were credited here and never typed until
cap     2026-08-30.
run     devprc -h
wait    8
run     ptxminst -?
wait    8
run     t
wait    8
run     t_trtest
wait    8

shot    dinfo
cap     Device information for the volume this collection is mounted as.
run     dinfo /dd
wait    3
run     shdev
wait    3

shot    dload
for     dload break sbreak setfont trap
cap     `dload' does NOT send a file down a serial line, whatever the name
cap     and DOC/INDEX said: its own usage line, below, says it loads a data
cap     file INTO A DATA MODULE.  `break' and `sbreak' are the serial-line
cap     examples -- assembler ones from the forums, harmless here because
cap     there is no serial line.  `break' has to be asked for by path: it
cap     is a bash builtin too, and the builtin answers first.  It prints
cap     nothing whatever it is given, which is why sbreak is shown as well.
cap     `trap' is the system-state trap-handler example and cannot install
cap     one from user state.  It must be asked for BY PATH: `trap' is a bash
cap     builtin, and the builtin answers first and silently -- which on
cap     2026-08-29 was briefly mistaken here for the program succeeding.
cap     Loading `math' first, which is the handler it names, does not
cap     change the outcome; this card loads it to show that.
run     dload -?
wait    6
run     /dd/CMDS/break -?
wait    4
run     sbreak -?
wait    6
run     load /dd/CMDS/math
wait    4
run     /dd/CMDS/trap
wait    6
run     setfont < /nil
wait    8

shot    drop
for     drop keep kept
cap     drop puts back exactly what keep wrote, and refuses to remove any
cap     file whose checksum has changed since.  Nothing has been kept here,
cap     which is what it says.
run     drop -n hack
wait    8

shot    e
cap     SEDT, the VMS-style editor, with VT220 keys.
run     e /dd/SRC/keep/about.c
wait    6
quit    \033\033

shot    em
for     em new_e e sedt
cap     em is MicroEMACS 3.8b, from EFFO forum 3, opened on a file -- the
cap     mode line names the version and the file.
run     em /dd/SYS/motd
wait    8
quit    \033
run     new_e < /nil
wait    10
run     sedt < /nil
wait    10

shot    every
for     every repeat cron bootlogger loglist
cap     every and repeat run a command again and again -- the two halves of
cap     cron for a machine that is not left running.  repeat takes a count,
cap     so it can be shown finishing; every runs until stopped, so it is
cap     asked to describe itself instead.  loglist is the login logger:
cap     `-i' records an arrival, `-l' prints the table.  Its display drops
cap     the leading character of every line after the first, which is its
cap     own fault and not the emulator's -- the bytes it writes are right.
cap     `bootlogger' answers nothing at all, even to `-?'; that blank is
cap     what it does, measured, not a capture that failed.
run     repeat 3 /dd/CMDS/date
wait    8
run     every -?
wait    6
run     cron -?
wait    6
run     bootlogger -?
wait    5
run     rm -f /dd/SYS/loglist
wait    4
run     tee </nil >/dd/SYS/loglist
wait    4
run     loglist -i
wait    5
run     loglist -o
wait    5
run     loglist -l
wait    8

shot    fastcc
for     fastcc compiler assembler lfmaker
cap     The GSHELL front ends, and fastcc -- a quicker driver for cc.
run     fastcc -?
wait    6
run     compiler -?
wait    6
run     assembler < /nil
wait    8
run     lfmaker < /nil
wait    10

shot    flink
cap     flink lists a module's links -- what has it loaded and who holds it.
run     flink /dd/CMDS/today
wait    5

shot    gen
for     gen suse about
cap     gen writes the FRAME of a new C program -- header block, authorship
cap     and version lines, and the sectioned comments a Microware example
cap     was laid out with.  It appends `.c' to whatever name you give it,
cap     which is why it looks as though it wrote nothing: `gen -p frame'
cap     leaves `frame.c'.  `-m' does a module frame, `-t' a type, `-f' a
cap     function declaration.
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/demo.c
wait    4
run     gen -p /dd/tmp/demo
wait    6
run     head -n 14 /dd/tmp/demo.c 2>/nil
wait    8
run     suse < /nil
wait    8

shot    getsys
cap     getsys walks OS-9's whole global page, field by field -- and under
cap     os9exec most of those fields are not kept, so what comes back is the
cap     emulator saying `unimplemented' for each one.  This is a picture of
cap     where the emulation stops, not of a broken program.
run     getsys | head -n 21
wait    6

shot    hinterhalt
for     hinterhalt testibc sqrtx bigsetter i_am_i
cap     hinterhalt, a small game that came with EFFO forum 7 -- one of the
cap     example programs the German OS-9 user group circulated.  Four more
cap     from the same shelf are asked for beside it -- `testibc' exercises
cap     the inter-process bus, `sqrtx' takes a square root, `bigsetter'
cap     demonstrates Modula-2 set operations and `i_am_i' reports its own
cap     process.  All four were credited here and never typed until
cap     2026-08-30.
run     hinterhalt
wait    10
run     testibc
wait    8
run     sqrtx 144
wait    8
run     bigsetter
wait    8
run     i_am_i
wait    8

shot    i_am_i
cap     i_am_i prints its own source: a Pascal quine.
run     i_am_i
wait    5

shot    ident
for     modinfo version mexist flink names liborder.os9
cap     Four ways of asking about a MODULE rather than a file: its header,
cap     its edition, whether it is loaded, and what is inside a library.
run     version /dd/CMDS/tar
wait    5
run     mexist bash
wait    5
run     mexist nosuchmodule
wait    5
run     liborder.os9 -?
wait    6

shot    isam
cap     The indexed-sequential file demonstration from the same shelf.
run     isam
wait    8

shot    keep
cap     keep is how a program leaves this disk for your own: it copies the
cap     binary AND whatever it needs.  Dry run first, which is what -n is
cap     for -- nothing is written here.
run     keep -n hack
wait    6

shot    kept
cap     kept lists what has been taken and what it came to.
run     kept
wait    5

shot    launch
for     launch dpark pri signal snd_sig suspend
cap     `launch' is NOT the background launcher its name and DOC/INDEX
cap     suggested: it is a LOGIN helper -- set the environment for the
cap     terminal type from SYS/config, optionally a default PATH and the
cap     emacs bindings, then start the shell you name.  `pri' changes a
cap     process's priority and answers nothing to -?; `signal' sends a
cap     signal, after a delay if you ask.
cap     `dpark' parks a disk's heads, `snd_sig' sends a signal by number
cap     and `suspend' stops a process; the three were credited here and
cap     never typed until 2026-08-30.
run     launch -?
wait    6
run     dpark -?
wait    6
run     snd_sig -?
wait    6
run     suspend -?
wait    6
run     pri -?
wait    6
run     signal -?
wait    6

shot    orbit
size    36 80
for     orbit
cap     The N3EMO orbit simulator tracking AO-13 over Pittsburgh for a day
cap     in March 1990 -- bearing and elevation, doppler shift, range,
cap     height, the sub-satellite point, and the operating mode the
cap     satellite is in at each sample.  It was recorded here as wanting an
cap     element file no archive carried.  All three files it wants are on
cap     the disk, in DOC/orbit, and it opens them by BARE NAME in the DATA
cap     DIRECTORY.  ON YOUR OWN OS-9 SYSTEM THAT IS TWO LINES -- `chd
cap     /dd/DOC/orbit' and `orbit' -- and nothing needs copying anywhere.
cap     Under os9exec neither shell will do it: bash's `cd' tracks a string
cap     and never moves the data directory, and `sh' cannot launch a
cap     program here at all.  So this card brings the three files to the
cap     directory it is already in, which is the bash workaround and not
cap     the recipe.  kepler.dat is the satellite database, pgh.sit the
cap     observing site, mode.dat the transponder schedule that fills the
cap     last column; bern.sit and zuerich.sit ship too.  Nothing is shipped
cap     at the disk root for this -- raw data files do not belong there.
cap     `nasa' is the one whose data really is user-supplied: it converts
cap     NASA two-line elements, which come from usenet.
run     cat /dd/DOC/orbit/kepler.dat > /dd/kepler.dat
wait    8
run     cat /dd/DOC/orbit/mode.dat > /dd/mode.dat
wait    8
run     cat /dd/DOC/orbit/pgh.sit > /dd/pgh.sit
wait    8
run     orbit
wait    12
send    d\r
wait    5
send    pgh\r
wait    5
send    3 10 90\r
wait    5
send    0\r
wait    5
send    1\r
wait    5
send    10\r
wait    5
send    \r
wait    25

shot    lunisolar
for     lunisolar ephem881 nasa
cap     The rest of the astronomy set.  `lunisolar' gives the phase of the
cap     moon in one line, or a whole lunisolar calendar as LaTeX if you
cap     give it a year and a time zone.  `nasa' converts NASA two-line
cap     keplerians into the format `orbit' reads, and wants a nasa.dat of
cap     your own -- those are posted to usenet, not shipped.  `ephem'
cap     proper is the one with its database on the disk, and has its own
cap     card; `orbit' has one now too.
run     lunisolar
wait    8
run     lunisolar -?
wait    8
run     nasa -?
wait    8
run     ephem881 < /nil
wait    10

shot    makecrc
for     makecrc
cap     makecrc is a GENERATOR: it writes CRC table source for six
cap     polynomials into the current directory and says nothing at all.
cap     The files it left are the output.
run     makecrc
wait    10
run     ls -l arc.c binhex.c ccitt.c kermit.c zip.c
wait    8

shot    map
cap     map shows the blocks a file occupies -- not a memory map, whatever
cap     the name suggests.
run     map -e /dd/DOC/INDEX
wait    5

shot    modinfo
cap     An OS-9 module header read back: name, type, size, edition and CRC.
cap     Every program on this disk is a module like this one.
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/today
wait    3

shot    mshell
cap     mshell wants a menu file, cannot find one, says so twice and runs
cap     anyway.  The error it reports for that failure is #255:255, which
cap     is not an OS-9 error code at all.
run     mshell
wait    4
send    echo mshell is running\r
wait    3

shot    names
cap     names lists the modules inside a file.  os9lib is the FORTRAN
cap     runtime, and it holds many.
run     names /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    5
kill

shot    ob68kdemo
cap     OmniBasic 1.16, a BASIC compiler, in its demonstration build.
run     ob68kdemo
wait    8

shot    oskversion
cap     What the system says it is.  getsys walks the whole global page, and
cap     every field os9exec does not keep answers `F$SetSys: unimplemented'
cap     -- so this screen is a picture of the emulator's edges.
run     oskversion
wait    2
run     sysid
wait    2
run     getsys
wait    4

shot    passwd
for     passwd who adduser
cap     passwd changes a password in SYS/password.  It matches on the user
cap     NAME, and the file is plain text you can read here.
run     cat /dd/SYS/password
wait    6
run     passwd -?
wait    6
run     adduser -?
wait    8
run     who < /nil
wait    8

shot    phone
cap     `phone' is not an address book: it connects two terminals over a
cap     communication path, and with none named it says so.  DOC/INDEX
cap     called it a phone-number book until this screen was taken.
run     phone
wait    5

shot    rndir
for     rndir mvolformat rendsk
cap     rndir renames a directory -- OS-9's `rename' would not touch one,
cap     so this existed.  Made, renamed and listed in a scratch directory
cap     of its own.
run     mkdir /dd/tmp/rnd
wait    5
run     mkdir /dd/tmp/rnd/olddir
wait    5
run     dir /dd/tmp/rnd
wait    6
run     rndir /dd/tmp/rnd/olddir /dd/tmp/rnd/newdir
wait    6
run     dir /dd/tmp/rnd
wait    6
run     mvolformat < /nil
wait    8
run     rendsk < /nil
wait    8

shot    scsiutil
for     scsiutil expreserve
cap     scsiutil talks to a SCSI device, and expreserve is vi's crash
cap     recovery.  Neither has anything to work on here, and both say so.
cap     This card credited four more programs until 2026-08-29 -- dedit,
cap     exrecover, read_mail and add_errmsg -- and typed none of them, so
cap     their "sample output" was scsiutil's usage message.  They are on
cap     the card below now, which runs each of them.
run     scsiutil -?
wait    8
run     expreserve -?
wait    8

shot    vi-recovery
size    36 80
for     dedit exrecover read_mail add_errmsg
cap     The four programs that used to be credited to the scsiutil card
cap     without ever being run.  `exrecover' brings back a vi buffer that
cap     expreserve saved and there is none, so it says what it wanted.
cap     `add_errmsg' builds vi's error-message file -- /dd/SYS/vi_errmsg is
cap     already on the disk.  `read_mail' is NOT vi's helper despite
cap     sitting beside these: it is a small mail reader of its own.
cap     `dedit' is Carl Kreider's BASIC09 disk sector editor and needs
cap     runb, like the other three BASIC09 programs here.
run     exrecover
wait    8
run     add_errmsg -?
wait    8
run     read_mail
wait    8
run     dedit
wait    8

shot    sddemo
cap     White's Speedisk 2.10, a disk defragmenter, drawing the map of a
cap     volume it is about to work on.
run     sddemo
wait    10

shot    shdev
for     shdev dinfo ssl dam
cap     shdev prints its heading and no devices: os9exec keeps no OS-9
cap     device table for it to walk.  dinfo, underneath, asks the device
cap     itself and answers properly -- 259 MB, 46% used.
run     shdev
wait    8
run     dinfo /dd
wait    8

shot    spline
cap     spline fits a curve through points and writes PostScript -- the
cap     `%!' at the head of the line is the PostScript magic.  It writes
cap     the same stream whatever it is given, including -?, so it never
cap     tells you what it wants.
run     echo "0 0" > /dd/tmp/pts
wait    2
run     spline /dd/tmp/pts | head -n 14
wait    5

shot    sqrtx
cap     A square-root demonstration -- one of the small programs the forums
cap     circulated to prove a compiler worked.
run     sqrtx
wait    6

shot    ssl
cap     ssl asks RBF for a file's segment list -- the sectors it was given,
cap     in order.  It prints the heading and no rows for every file tried
cap     here: os9exec's RBF does not hand back the file descriptor sector,
cap     so there is nothing for ssl to list.  `map -e' answers the same
cap     question and does work; it has its own card.
run     ssl /dd/DOC/INDEX
wait    4

shot    sysid
for     sysid sysmax sysmin oskversion
cap     sysid asks the kernel to identify itself with F$SysID, which
cap     os9exec does not implement -- so what comes back is the emulator
cap     saying so, field by field.  The numbers below it are real.
run     sysid
wait    6
run     sysmax
wait    5
run     sysmin
wait    5

shot    sysmon
cap     sysmon draws memory and process state on a real machine, and it
cap     will not start here: it reads the system's version number and
cap     refuses anything older than it was built for.  os9exec reports
cap     V4.0, which sysmon 6.1 considers too old.  `mfree', `map' and
cap     `free' are what answer the same questions on this disk.
run     sysmon
wait    8

shot    top
cap     top prints its column headings and then aborts -- every time, with
cap     no invocation that gets past them.  It is one of the eighteen
cap     crashes in DOC/STATUS.  `procs' is not on this disk and `aprocs'
cap     aborts the same way; `map' and `free' are what is left.
run     top
wait    6

shot    transfer
for     transfer trunc preset fillup vlen
cap     `trunc' cuts a file short and the listing either side of it is the
cap     whole demonstration.  `transfer', which names this card, copies
cap     from GDOS DISKS to OS-9 and takes no options at all -- not the
cap     general device-to-device copier DOC/INDEX called it; `cp' and
cap     `dsave' are those.  `vlen' in this group is
cap     this group is NOT a reporting tool whatever DOC/INDEX said: it is
cap     a variable-length-record demonstration that builds a filesystem of
cap     its own and fills it.  `fillup' pads a file out to a length with a
cap     constant byte -- `-n=' the length and `-i=' the byte -- and says
cap     how many it added, which is the last two lines here.
run     cp /dd/SYS/holidays /dd/tmp/cut.txt
wait    6
run     ls -l /dd/tmp/cut.txt
wait    5
run     trunc /dd/tmp/cut.txt 100
wait    6
run     ls -l /dd/tmp/cut.txt
wait    6
run     fillup -n=200 -i=65 /dd/tmp/cut.txt
wait    6
run     ls -l /dd/tmp/cut.txt
wait    6
run     preset < /nil
wait    8
run     transfer < /nil
wait    8

shot    tty
for     tty vecho clear lmargin
cap     tty names the terminal; vecho writes without a newline; lmargin
cap     sets a left margin on what follows.
run     tty
wait    5
run     vecho "no newline here -- "
wait    5
run     lmargin -?
wait    6

shot    ub68kdemo
for     ub68kdemo ub68020demo
cap     UniBasic 1.10 -- the other BASIC compiler here, same arrangement.
cap     It ships twice, once for the 68000 and once for the 68020, and
cap     BOTH run: each announces its own processor, which is the only line
cap     that differs.  The 020 build was filed as saying nothing until
cap     2026-08-29, and it never did.
run     ub68kdemo | head -n 3
wait    8
run     ub68020demo | head -n 3
wait    8

shot    umacs
cap     MicroEMACS, editing the collection's own C source.  The mode line
cap     at the foot is emacs's, and the keys are emacs's.
run     umacs /dd/SRC/keep/keep.c
wait    6
quit    \030\003

shot    vc
for     vc
cap     `vc' is not a visual compare at all: it is a SPREADSHEET -- "Welcome
cap     to the Spreadsheet Calculator, type ? for help" -- with rows and
cap     columns and a formula line.  DOC/INDEX called it "visual compare of
cap     two files" until this screen was taken.  It also does not answer
cap     Ctrl-E, so it is quit properly here with `q'.
run     vc
wait    8
quit    q\r

shot    version
cap     version and mexist ask about a module rather than a file.
run     version /dd/CMDS/today
wait    3
run     mexist bash
wait    3

shot    vlen
cap     vlen reports a file's record length; trunc cuts a file to size.
run     vlen /dd/DOC/INDEX
wait    4
run     vlen /dd/CMDS/today
wait    4

shot    yagi
cap     yagi designs a Yagi antenna to DL6WU's method: give it a frequency,
cap     an element count, a boom diameter and a tube size, and it works out
cap     the lengths and spacings.  144 MHz, nine elements, 20 mm boom.
run     yagi
wait    4
send    144\r
wait    3
send    9\r
wait    4
send    20\r
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    5
send    4\r
wait    10


shot    modules
for     cio csl csl020 math math881 os9lib rtfdat
cap     The runtime modules the starred programs need.  They are not
cap     programs -- you cannot run them -- so this is what they look like
cap     to `modinfo': name, type, edition and CRC.  cio and math ship here
cap     by Microware's permission; SOURCES.txt records the exchange.
cap     All seven are asked for below, one line each; three were until
cap     2026-08-29 and the rest stood behind them.
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/cio | head -n 6
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/csl | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/csl020 | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/math | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/math881 | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/os9lib | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/rtfdat | grep -a "name\|Edition\|size"
wait    12


shot    drivers
for     keydrv.mm1 msdrv.901_340 msdrv_340.901.ms rb37c65 scsi_mm1a snddrv windio.52
cap     The MM/1 drivers -- keyboard, mouse, floppy, SCSI, sound and the
cap     windowing terminal.  They are device drivers for hardware that is
cap     not here, so again `modinfo' is what there is to show: type $C
cap     (device driver) rather than $1 (program).
cap     Each of the six is asked for, one line apiece, rather than one of
cap     them standing for all six as it did until 2026-08-29.
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/keydrv.mm1 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/msdrv.901_340 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/rb37c65 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/scsi_mm1a | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/snddrv | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/windio.52 | head -n 4
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/MM1/msdrv_340.901.ms | head -n 4
wait    12

shot    load
cap     load puts a MODULE in the module directory so that a program which
cap     LINKS it can find it.  `for', the RTF Fortran driver, prints nothing
cap     at all until os9lib is loaded -- and then it says what it takes.  It
cap     is called by its full path because `for' is a bash keyword and the
cap     shell would answer first.
cap     That silence is the whole symptom, and this is the cure: a
cap     clean-room load, contributed by the os9exec project and built here,
cap     with its source in SRC/load.
run     /dd/CMDS/for
wait    6
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/for
wait    8
run     load /dd/CMDS/nosuchthing
wait    8


shot    biory
for     biory creadoc
cap     biory is the RTF Fortran example: a biorhythm program that prompts
cap     in German, once os9lib is loaded.  creadoc, beside it, pulls the
cap     documentation comments out of Fortran source.
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    8
run     biory
wait    8
send    Tester\r
wait    6


shot    modules2
for     ptxm wn.stb
cap     Two more modules that are not programs: `ptxm', Nick Holgate's Path
cap     Table eXtension Module, which `ptxminst' installs, and `wn.stb', the
cap     symbol table that ships beside the WN web server.
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/ptxm | head -n 12
wait    12
run     modinfo /dd/CMDS/NETWORK/wn.stb | head -n 4
wait    10

shot    memtools
for     loadmem savemem snap scriptmaster
cap     Three that read or write something the shell cannot reach.  loadmem
cap     puts a file into memory at an address and savemem writes a block of
cap     memory out; both say plainly that you must be the super user and
cap     that the addresses are hex.  `snap' photographs the terminal into a
cap     file -- called `polaroid' if you do not name one.  `scriptmaster'
cap     prints nothing whatever it is given.
run     loadmem -?
wait    8
run     savemem -?
wait    8
run     snap -?
wait    8
run     scriptmaster < /nil
wait    8

shot    mtst
for     mtst
cap     mtst exercises the C maths library and prints what each function
cap     returned beside what it was given -- ceil, floor and round on the
cap     same number, integer and floating side by side.  It is one of the
cap     small programs a port was checked with.
run     mtst | head -n 16
wait    12

shot    fontgen
for     fontgen
cap     fontgen writes the SOURCE of a character font: an OS-9 data module
cap     in assembler, psect and all, ready for the 80-column card it was
cap     written for.  It reads its bitmap from standard input and will sit
cap     waiting on the terminal if you forget to give it one.
run     fontgen < /nil | head -n 10
wait    12

shot    silent
for     blackjack X11R6shl pdraw readnews
cap     The last few with nothing much to show, and two of them turned out
cap     to have something after all.  `readnews' RUNS -- it opens the news
cap     reader and says there are no newsgroups, which is the truth on a
cap     disk with no spool.  `pdraw' prints its configuration before it
cap     looks for an X server.  `blackjack' is one of the three BASIC09
cap     programs here: it lives in CMDS/BROKEN, off the login PATH, and
cap     wants `runb'.  `X11R6shl' is not a program at all, and bash says
cap     so.  `readnews' goes last here because it does not stop asking:
cap     with nothing on its standard input it puts the same question over
cap     and over, so the card takes one answer and ends it.
run     pdraw < /nil | head -n 4
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/BROKEN/blackjack
wait    6
run     X11R6shl
wait    6
run     readnews < /nil | head -n 2
wait    8
kill

